 11541                                  ; fs_load: reads the OS volume into nodes 0..OS_NODES. Tries device 0
 11542                                  ; (legacy ATA primary master) first, exactly as always. If that device
 11543                                  ; never responds at all - common on real hardware that has no PATA/IDE
 11544                                  ; controller and only exposes its disk(s) over SATA - it then looks
 11545                                  ; through whatever AHCI port slots (4..4+ahci_port_count-1) ahci_init
 11546                                  ; found at boot, preferring one that already holds a valid SFFS volume,
 11547                                  ; falling back to the first one that simply responds (a blank/new SATA
 11548                                  ; SSD) so a fresh filesystem still has somewhere to persist to. Whichever
 11549                                  ; device actually ends up holding the OS volume is remembered in
 11550                                  ; [boot_device] for fs_save (and cmd_format's "don't touch the boot
 11551                                  ; drive" check) to use afterwards. If nothing responds anywhere, falls
 11552                                  ; back to fs_init for a fresh in-memory-only filesystem.
 11553                                  ; Mounted volumes are never loaded here - you re-attach them after boot
 11554                                  ; with 'dscan' + 'mount'.
 11555                                  ; sets fs_loaded_from_disk, fs_disk_available and boot_device accordingly.
